INT. HYDROPONIC BAY, GENERATION SHIP HALCYON - SHIP'S NIGHT

Green racks, pink grow-lights, mist. A long table of lettuces. Four crew in coveralls prune and murmur.

OKONKWO, 40s, ties off a vine. LIRA PETROVA, 20s, weighs a tomato. DR. HAMASAKI, 60s, taps a tablet. TEODORO, 30s, wipes a lens. Nobody hurries.

OKONKWO
Third rack's leaning.

LIRA
The blue lamp's out.

TEODORO
I'll fix it.

DR. HAMASAKI
Don't fix it. Replace it.

A hatch hisses. A small GIRL, 12, ship-born, hair in two buns, steps in hugging a bucket. She sets the bucket down and every head turns.

OKONKWO
You're off-shift, little one.

GIRL
I brought worms.

TEODORO
What's your name again?

GIRL
Newt.

MBEKI, 50s, the bay chief, steps out from behind a rack with a pruning knife.

MBEKI
Newt. Mind the mist.

LIRA
(over Okonkwo)
Good worms.

TEODORO
(over Lira)
How many, Newt?

DR. HAMASAKI
Don't count them, Teodoro. Plant them.

NEWT
They don't mind.

DR. HAMASAKI
I'm sure they don't, Newt.

Okonkwo smiles, ties another vine.
